 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization is a screen-space rendering method that projects a set of 3D Gaussian primitives into 2D ellipses and alpha-composites them to produce an RGB image [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time, high-fidelity rendering of scenes represented by discrete, point-based splats, distinguishing itself from volumetric ray-marching methods like NeRF by using explicit geometric primitives and from standard OpenGL rasterization by computing analytic gradients for the non-linear projection and compositing operations [S6, unofficial][S10]. It builds on [Rendering Implementation](7155-rendering-implementation.md) by implementing the specific forward and backward passes required for optimization.
+3D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization is a screen-space rendering method that projects a set of 3D Gaussian primitives into 2D ellipses and alpha-composites them to produce an RGB image [S1][S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time, high-fidelity rendering of learned scene representations by replacing triangle rasterization with Gaussian splatting [S4, unofficial]. Unlike volumetric ray-marching methods like NeRF, it uses discrete, point-based splatting, and unlike standard OpenGL rasterization, it computes analytic gradients for the non-linear projection and compositing operations, enabling end-to-end differentiable training [S3, S6, unofficial]. It builds on [Rendering Implementation](7155-rendering-implementation.md) by implementing the specific forward and backward passes required for optimization.
 
